🚀 Week 5 – Day 5 Try-Catch Blocks, final, finally & finalize() ☕

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Java provides several mechanisms for:

✔ Handling exceptions
✔ Controlling inheritance and modification
✔ Cleaning up resources

Although `final`, `finally`, and `finalize()` sound similar, they serve completely different purposes.

👉 This is a very common Java interview topic.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🛠️ 1. try-catch Blocks

Used to handle exceptions and prevent program crashes.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="tc1"
try {
    // risky code
}
catch(Exception e) {
    // exception handling
}
```

---

📌 Example

```java id="tc2"
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {
            int result = 10 / 0;
            System.out.println(result);
        }
        catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
        }
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text id="tc3"
Cannot divide by zero
```

---

🔹 Multiple Catch Blocks

```java id="tc4"
try {
    String str = null;
    System.out.println(str.length());
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Arithmetic Error");
}
catch (NullPointerException e) {
    System.out.println("Null Error");
}
```

---

🔹 Nested Try Block

```java id="tc5"
try {
    try {
        int a = 10 / 0;
    }
    catch (ArithmeticException e) {
        System.out.println("Inner Catch");
    }
}
catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Outer Catch");
}
```

